                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Honestly, I don't understand how this version is supposed to be the definitive way to experience Dead Island. Sure, the textures, character models and environmental assets have been upgraded, and at first glance the game looks noticeably better. But once you actually start playing, you realise just how much of the original experience has been lost.\n\nThe most disappointing part is the gore and overall brutality. The original Dead Island had much more satisfying damage effects, dismemberment and enemy reactions. The Definitive Edition feels noticeably toned down in comparison, and combat simply doesn't have the same visceral impact anymore.\n\nAnother thing I really dislike is the zombies themselves. In the original, enemies had much more visual variety and personality. You could actually distinguish different zombies by their appearance. In the Definitive Edition, they seem to have lost a lot of that individuality. They feel incredibly generic, with barely any visual distinction between them. It makes the world feel less believable and the enemies less memorable.\n\nThe physics and ragdolls are another massive downgrade. The original had wonderfully satisfying physics, with zombies reacting much more dynamically to your attacks. Heavy hits actually felt heavy, bodies behaved more naturally, and combat had a certain unpredictability that made it fun. The Definitive Edition feels noticeably less impressive in this regard, with weaker reactions and less convincing corpse physics.\n\nEven the lighting and atmosphere have suffered. Despite the upgraded assets, the original had a much more distinctive visual identity. Its vibrant tropical environments created an amazing contrast with the horrifying zombie outbreak. The Definitive Edition somehow manages to lose some of that charm, with different colour grading, questionable lighting and visual inconsistencies. Even the water looks worse in certain comparisons.\n\nThere are also animation issues and missing little details that make the game feel less polished than it should. Some environmental interactions have been lost, and even smaller things, like the original displaying quest rewards before accepting side missions, are missing.\n\nDon't get me wrong, the improved textures and character models are nice, but better graphics don't automatically make a better game. A remaster should improve the original without sacrificing what made it special in the first place.\n\nPersonally, I'd take the original Dead Island over the Definitive Edition any day. It might look older, but the atmosphere, gore, physics, ragdolls and overall feeling of the game are simply superior.\n\nA prettier game doesn't necessarily mean a better game. Unfortunately, Dead Island Definitive Edition is a perfect example of that.",
